And one of them is that you don't need to
go to the hospital to get the treatment, which

00:01:58.369 --> 00:02:01.140
is the case with all the insects so far.

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:05.479
But you can get the call if that could be
a game changer.

00:02:05.479 --> 00:02:09.720
The compound that you're are talking about
and you said, well, the numbers it is a protease

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:12.641
inhibitor is a good thing.

00:02:12.641 --> 00:02:13.641
It is.

00:02:13.641 --> 00:02:20.650
This is also the first molecule that is coming
from this type of class.

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:23.950
This is a good thing because you can combine
it with other.

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:31.970
Also, the mechanism of action is such that
it's not expected to be subject to mutations,

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:35.030
particularly because it's not acting on the
spike.

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:40.470
As we all know, all the mutations that we
are hearing right now are changes in the province

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:41.470
of the spine.

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:43.850
Speaker 1: So this is not a trivial detail.

00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:49.900
An antiviral like what is being developed
by Pfizer would not be as jeopardized by mutations

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:50.900
and variants.

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:52.220
That's really important.

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:56.620
And of course, if all goes as described and
there's still much to be determined here,

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:58.130
this could be a real game changer.

00:02:58.130 --> 00:03:03.670
Now, I have to tell you, it is not lost on
me that as Albert Bourla is speaking, the

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:08.760
stock price of Pfizer is on the screen as
their CEO is talking about medications they

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:10.200
may soon have.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:14.560
This is a really stark reminder of the system
that we have, and I have addressed this before.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:17.280
I believe there has to be a middle ground
here.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:22.220
On the one hand, we can't allow pharmaceutical
companies to hold us hostage when it comes

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:23.370
to our health.

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:27.340
On the other hand, I don't expect the best
and brightest minds to line up to solve our

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:29.730
problems as a charitable contribution.

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:31.650
I just don't expect that to happen.

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:37.600
The creators of these treatments, vaccines,
et cetera, should be rewarded.

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:41.650
They really have done something incredible
that the Turkish couple in Germany who developed

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:49.650
what is now called the Pfizer vaccine, they
make a profit by doing this, they will then

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:54.840
be likely to do it again and they will be
more likely to come up with other cures, treatments,

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:56.960
vaccines when the next pandemic comes around.

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:01.060
If there is no profit now, who will invest
next time?

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:04.480
For now, Pfizer has increased revenue about
two percent.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:12.390
After investing about 10 billion researching
the vaccine, they took some risk of investing

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:13.480
in a covered vaccine.

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:17.400
They could have done oncology stuff which
has higher profit margin.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:18.919
The same applies to the researchers.

00:04:18.919 --> 00:04:22.060
The founders of biotech are now really wealthy.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:25.979
But don't we want people to enter the field
of science and develop things that can be

00:04:25.979 --> 00:04:26.979
lifesaving?

00:04:26.979 --> 00:04:29.430
But we need to ensure access.

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:31.690
We need to look at government subsidies.

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:36.889
And there there's a concept in health care
where premiums can't exceed benefits by a

00:04:36.889 --> 00:04:42.270
certain percentage or a portion of the premiums
have to be refunded to the consumer.

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:47.490
Maybe allowing pharmaceutical companies to
have a margin that's fair, but limited would

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:48.490
work.

00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:49.490
I don't know.

00:04:49.490 --> 00:04:50.990
I'm just thinking out loud.

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:55.889
There are certain people I don't mind seeing
being paid handsomely.

00:04:55.889 --> 00:04:56.889
Right, Mike?

00:04:56.889 --> 00:05:03.120
If I had kids, my kids, teachers and doctors,
firefighters, people develop developing vaccines

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:04.870
to deal with a pandemic.

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:07.289
Should the government be involved in determining
a fair price?

00:05:07.289 --> 00:05:08.849
Yeah, maybe so.

00:05:08.849 --> 00:05:10.659
But context is crucial here.

00:05:10.659 --> 00:05:14.539
Bernie Sanders made a statement about this
and he made a statement about vaccine production

00:05:14.539 --> 00:05:20.150
and delivery outside the US and allowing other
countries to use the IP, the intellectual

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:22.479
property of US companies to make vaccines.

00:05:22.479 --> 00:05:26.259
And instead of having to wait around for the
U.S. companies, those are great ideas.

00:05:26.259 --> 00:05:27.900
The government should be involved.

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:34.880
But in a sense, I am more pleased with people
involved in biomedical research making money

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:35.999
than in some other areas.

00:05:35.999 --> 00:05:38.410
Now, does the CEO deserve 18 million?

00:05:38.410 --> 00:05:44.439
That's a very different question and one that
that I think we can fairly debate.

00:05:44.439 --> 00:05:50.889
And the counter is always, well, if you only
pay two million, then some mining company

00:05:50.889 --> 00:05:54.749
will swoop them up, sweep them up instead
of a pharmaceutical company, and maybe the

00:05:54.749 --> 00:05:59.999
pharmaceutical company will be slower and
OK, OK, there's maybe a debate there, but

00:05:59.999 --> 00:06:02.259
I think that it has to be a middle path here.

